{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Water to drink.</b> The implication of this is that they did not drink, for it does not say וישתו ('and they drank'); and if they did not drink, how could they endure seven days without water?" "<b>Rather,</b> it may be explained that they did drink, and לשתות ('to drink') is as it were וישתו ('and they drank'). And the language may be understood in two ways: that they did not drink and that they did drink. Its explanation is that they did not drink from what they dug, for even what they dug turned to blood; but it is possible that they drank from the brackish waters that are unfit for drinking — and this is what is said: <b>for they could not drink of the waters of the river,</b> which were sweet and fit for drinking. Thus explained the Gaon Rabbeinu Saadiah, of blessed memory. And he explained that there is a difference between מימי ('waters of') and מי ('waters of'), for the form מימי is used of waters fit to drink, as in (Numbers 20) ונתן מימיו, (Exodus 23) וברך את לחמך ואת מימיך, and as it is written (II Chronicles 32) יחזקיהו סתם את מוצא מימי גיחון, and likewise (Lamentations 5) מימינו בכסף שתינו. And the form מי is used of waters unfit to drink, as in what is said of the flood (Genesis 7) מפני מי המבול, (Isaiah 54) מעבור מי נח, and likewise of the sea it is written (Exodus 15) וישב ה׳ את מי הים, (Deuteronomy 11) אשר הציף את מי ים סוף על פניהם, and likewise (Jeremiah 2) מה לך לדרך מצרים לשתות מי שיחור ומה לך לדרך אשור לשתות מי נהר — for they were rivers unfit for drinking. And we find regarding the Jordan that Scripture mentions both forms, מימי and מי, because some of the waters of the Jordan were fit for drinking and some were not."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:53.621812303Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}